---
import ForceComposition from "../../../components/studies/ForceComposition.svelte";
import MovementBench from "../../../components/studies/MovementBench.svelte";
import PlasmaEscape from "../../../components/studies/PlasmaEscape.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "plasma-behaviour-2026-11-01",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section aria-labelledby="closure">
    <h2 id="closure">Closed / Unconcluded and broken</h2>
    <p>
      Closed on 1 November 2026 at the designer’s request. The plasma design
      question remains unresolved. The designer judged the work after 02.2
      unsuccessful: repeated presentation changes and new tools did not produce
      useful design decisions. Technical checks do not override that assessment.
      The examples below are retained as evidence, not approved game behaviour.
    </p>
    <h3>Useful findings retained</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>02.1:</strong>
        the six-configuration comparison was accepted as useful; its delay and
        thrust contrast warranted investigation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>02.2:</strong>
        even 0.10 seconds was an interesting delay. Around 0.34 seconds and
        above, further delay made little visible handling difference under those
        particular acceleration, speed and input conditions. Neither number is a
        final setting.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Intermediate decision:</strong>
        diminishing speed returns and maximum speed belong to a generic chassis
        model, provisionally using the ion curve. This establishes a direction,
        not a validated mixed-engine implementation.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Integration candidates, not integration approval</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Generic chassis speed rule:</strong>
        carry the ownership decision into a future propulsion specification.
        Combined-force semantics and production behaviour still need a design
        conclusion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Short plasma delay:</strong>
        retain 0.10 seconds as a candidate for a future playable trial; do not
        replace the game’s delay on the strength of this study alone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plasma aft with ion sides:</strong>
        retain the escape-engine role as a candidate. Force, final delay, energy
        cost and charge decay remain unresolved.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No production tuning is promoted by closure. Stabilizers were a correction
      to the study fixtures, not a new game feature. The 2× plasma setting and
      the later force-composition examples were exploratory and were not
      accepted as balance or combination rules.
    </p>
    <h3>Follow-up brief</h3>
    <p>
      First review the extraction above for concrete integration scope. A
      separate flight-bench design study should start from the successful
      earlier presentations and establish predictable, understandable,
      comparable paths, input sequences, graphs, scales and controls before
      further engine exploration. No new bench is developed in this closed
      study.
    </p>
    <p>
      A later close-loop investigation should examine the sequence of requests,
      corrections and outcomes: why presentation changes and successive
      exploration steps displaced useful design discussion. Cause analysis and
      proposed factory lessons remain pending. The designer regards this failure
      as useful evidence for developing the design factory.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="purpose">
    <h2 id="purpose">01 / Committing to a burn, recovering from a mistake</h2>
    <p>
      A ship approaching a star needs to change its course before the close pass
      becomes a collision. Ion responds immediately to a correction. Plasma
      currently requires 0.6 seconds of held input before producing thrust;
      releasing the control or losing power resets that charge. A short tap can
      therefore spend energy without changing the ship’s course. With plasma on
      the side mounts, that delay also affects the start of a turn, while
      existing momentum and spin continue.
    </p>
    <p>
      The delay could be the price of performance ion cannot offer. Stronger
      acceleration after charging would reward a committed burn with a harder
      push; a higher top speed would reward sustaining it long enough to leave
      ion behind. These are different rewards: reaching a shared ceiling sooner
      changes short manoeuvres, while exceeding ion’s ceiling opens up faster
      travel and more demanding approaches. Plasma might offer either or both.
      The direction to investigate is a worthwhile exchange of immediate
      response for greater performance, with the reward and its magnitude still
      unsettled.
    </p>
    <p>
      This study will investigate whether that exchange makes plasma an
      appealing choice alongside ion, through launch, sustained flight and
      course correction. Comparisons should distinguish acceleration from top
      speed, and include release and reapplication so the cost of charging stays
      visible. Turning and recovery matter because the extra speed must remain
      usable. Charge duration, power cost and performance values remain open;
      the current 0.6-second delay is a baseline, not a settled target. Graphics
      and exhaust belong to the following study. Gravity, friction and the
      established ion behaviour provide the comparison baseline.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Where does delayed power belong?</h2>
    <h3>02.1 / Six configurations, one flight sequence</h3>
    <p>
      The six-configuration comparison is retained as the basis for further
      exploration. Its delay and thrust contrast is interesting enough to
      investigate; the current values are not yet final balance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compare three side pairs—Light ion, Heavy ion and Plasma—then four-engine
      builds with all Light ion, Light sides with Plasma aft, and all Plasma.
      Every ship is a Fighter with its actual fitted mass and stabilizers
      enabled. The same fourteen-second input sequence drives every path: LR,
      LR, coast, L, coast, LR, coast, R, coast, R, coast, LR, LR, LR; one second
      per phase. All start at rest at the same origin, facing right.
    </p>
    <p>
      Follow the coloured paths through turns and release, not only the final
      distance. The side-only trio compares immediate and delayed steering. The
      four-engine trio shows whether immediate ion steering makes delayed aft
      power useful, and what changes when every engine must charge. Current
      Plasma values are the common provisional baseline: 0.6-second charge,
      38,000 force per engine, mass 24 and 700 units/s ceiling. Ion uses the
      current production settings. This round compares configurations before
      tuning the reward.
    </p>
    <MovementBench
      plasma
      client:load
      label="Six plasma and ion configurations"
    />
    <p>
      Solid lines show speed, traversed path length and signed angular speed;
      positive rotation is clockwise. Dashed lines show the highest currently
      available engine-family ceiling. A mixed build still applies ion and
      plasma impulses separately: its dashed line is not a new combined limit.
      Zero means no engine is ready to thrust, not zero travel speed. Coasting
      retains travel momentum; stabilizers oppose uncommanded spin. Aft engines
      respond only to LR; release resets their charge, while an uninterrupted
      side engine keeps its own timer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bench uses production assembled-engine forces, mount geometry, mass,
      charge timers and free-fall rotation at 60 ticks/s with four substeps.
      Power is unlimited; gravity, drag, wrapping and randomness are absent.
      Each view shows the complete run with framing fitted to its paths. This
      compares configuration consequences, not survival endurance or final
      balance. The paths and graphs are static; time is read from the graph
      axes.
    </p>
    <h3 id="delay">02.2 / How much anticipation does plasma ask for?</h3>
    <p>
      Vary only charge delay from 0.10 to 0.99 seconds in 0.01-second steps.
      Keep the six loadouts, thrust, mass, ceiling and input sequence from 02.1.
      Both comparisons include production stabilizers. Every view draws the
      complete fourteen-second run; move the slider to compare paths and graphs.
    </p>
    <MovementBench
      plasma
      delayStudy
      client:load
      label="Plasma delay comparison"
    />
    <p>
      First inspect the left turn at 3–4 seconds, then the repeated right turns
      at 7–8 and 9–10 seconds. How much of each command is spent waiting, and
      what momentum carries through the coast afterward? Compare the mixed
      Light-side/Plasma-aft build with Plasma sides. Finish with the sustained
      11–14-second burn to see how the same delay affects its eventual payoff.
      Near 0.99 seconds, little of an isolated one-second command remains for
      the main plasma engine; stabilizers still respond immediately.
    </p>
    <p>
      The next decision is a promising delay range for subsequent thrust and
      speed experiments. This replay cannot establish how quickly a human can
      learn to anticipate the delay. Release still cuts thrust and resets charge
      immediately; charge retention and decay remain a separate investigation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Fix after 02.2:</strong>
      Stabilizers were missing from both comparisons and are now enabled. The
      earlier unassisted paths do not establish stabilized handling. Both views
      now draw complete static runs; the delay control is a 0.10–0.99-second
      slider. Framing and graph scales refit when the delay changes. The six
      configurations and the delay investigation remain the agreed scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Observation from 02.2:</strong>
      Even 0.10 seconds provides an interesting delay. Around 0.34 seconds and
      above, further delay made little visible difference to handling under
      these acceleration, speed and input conditions. This is a bounded design
      observation, not a universal threshold.
    </p>
    <h3 id="escape">02.3 / Plasma as an aft escape engine</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Intermediate decision:</strong>
      Diminishing speed returns and maximum speed move to the generic chassis
      model, outside plasma tuning. Use the ion curve provisionally for every
      chassis and combined engine force: 340 units/s at one Light-equivalent,
      40% maximum extra allowance, falloff 0.8. Its ceiling approaches 476
      units/s. Earlier experiments retain their original speed rules; this
      section applies the new shared rule locally.
    </p>
    <p>
      All four Fighters have two Light-ion side engines and one occupied aft
      slot: Light ion, Heavy ion, current-force Plasma, or adjustable Plasma.
      The slider changes only the last engine’s force from 1–10× Light, starting
      at 2×. Both Plasma references use the provisional 0.10-second delay and
      current mass. “Current force” means 38,000 force units (about 1.50×
      Light), not the old plasma-specific speed ceiling or 0.6-second delay.
    </p>
    <PlasmaEscape client:load />
    <p>
      Every run starts at rest with zero spin and follows the same
      fourteen-second input sequence as 02.1 and 02.2. The timeline and numbered
      path markers show where each burn, turn and coast happens. Aft thrust
      fires only during LR; both plasma engines charge for 0.10 seconds after
      each release. Compare acceleration, speed and traversed path length across
      the four builds.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stabilizers are enabled, including turning assistance and braking. Actual
      mass, production angular motion, charge timing and thrust taper are
      reused. Ready engine forces combine under the provisional generic chassis
      curve locally; the live game is unchanged. Paths share one fitted scale;
      graph scales stay fixed as force changes. Unlimited power, no gravity,
      drag, wrapping or randomness isolate propulsion. This does not establish
      escape success against a pursuer or gravity well. Energy balance and
      charge decay remain outside this experiment.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="force-composition">
    <h3 id="force-composition">
      02.4 / From engine force to ship acceleration
    </h3>
    <p>
      The paths in 02.3 made combined propulsion difficult to read. This bench
      separates engine force, ship mass and the diminishing speed allowance. It
      diagnoses the provisional rule already used in 02.3; it does not settle a
      new rule. More ready force raises acceleration from rest and also raises
      the ceiling, with diminishing returns.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with equal mass to isolate force addition, then enable fitted mass
      to see the weight tradeoff. Move the speed probe to compare how much
      forward acceleration remains at the same speed. Each configuration has its
      own graph with identical axes. Plasma uses the provisional 2× Light force;
      switch its charged state to expose the cost of waiting.
    </p>
    <ForceComposition client:load />
    <p>
      These are stripped-down fixtures: a 95-mass Fighter body plus only the
      listed engines, all pointing forward through its centre. They are not the
      complete builds from 02.3. Fixed mass is 115; fitted mass includes each
      engine even while it is inactive. There are no side engines, turning,
      gravity, drag or power limits. Stabilizers have nothing to correct in this
      aligned fixture.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculation is combined ready force ÷ mass, followed by the production
      speed taper. One Light supplies 25,300 force units; Heavy supplies 1.75×
      that. The shared ion-derived ceiling approaches 476 units/s and currently
      depends on force, not mass. The graphs show forward acceleration, not
      elapsed time or distance. A charged-state switch replaces a timed delay
      here so force composition can be inspected independently.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>03 / Readiness not established</h2>
    <p>
      The study closed during exploration. No final plasma behaviour was
      selected for readiness assessment.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>04 / Integration not planned</h2>
    <p>
      The extraction at the top records candidates for separate review. This
      study provides no approved production integration plan.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
